{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-23236",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14253746/assets/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nLet's first find the value of angle $2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ where $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\n$$\n2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{2}=2(\\frac{\\pi }{3})+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}).$ Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6})=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ \n\nUsing the fact that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }$ and $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6})}=\\frac{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}{(-\\frac{1}{2})}=\\sqrt{3}, \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6})}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6})}=\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{2})}{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we combine the results:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6})+\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6})=\\sqrt{3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}=\\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\n$$",
